=====================================================================================================================
Flags

Flag	Name	          Meaning
ZF	  Zero Flag	      The result was zero.
CF	  Carry Flag	    An unsigned carry or borrow occurred.
SF	  Sign Flag	      The result's most significant bit is 1.
OF	  Overflow Flag	  Signed arithmetic overflow occurred.
PF	  Parity Flag	    The low byte of the result has even parity.
AF	  Auxiliary Carry	Carry between bits 3 and 4; mainly relevant to BCD-related instructions.
=====================================================================================================================
cmp destination, source

the CPU calculates the subtraction: R = destination - source
to update the status flags, but it discards R
--------------------
mov eax, 5
cmp eax, 5

5 - 5 = 0 => ZF = 1. A subsequent "je" will jump, while a subsequent "jne" will not.
--------------------
mov eax, 5
cmp eax, 8

The subtraction is conceptually 5 - 8 = -3

For a 32-bit subtraction, the result is represented modulo 2^32: -3 mod 2^32 = 2^32 - 3

Its bit pattern is 0xFFFFFFFD. Thus, SF = 1 and ZF = 0. For this subtraction, CF = 1 and OF = 0 as well.
Notice that a negative mathematical result is not stored by cmp; 
it is the conceptual subtraction result that determines the flags.

|-----------------------------------------------|
| Instruction	    Equivalent alias	  Condition |
| JE	            JZ	                ZF = 1    |
| JNE	            JNZ	                ZF = 0    |
|-----------------------------------------------|
=====================================================================================================================
TEST performs a bitwise AND but discards the result. It updates flags based on that result, clearing CF and OF.

test eax, eax
jz   is_zero

If EAX is zero: 0 AND 0=0: so ZF = 1 and jz jumps.

If EAX is nonzero, at least one bit is set, so the AND result is nonzero, ZF = 0, and the jump isn't taken.
